var e=[{id:"dns-internet",title:"DNS & How the Internet Works",category:"full-stack-lifecycle",order:1,explanation:`The Domain Name System (DNS) is the phonebook of the internet. When a user types a URL, the browser queries a DNS resolver that walks the hierarchy: root servers, TLD servers (.com, .org), and authoritative name servers to resolve the domain to an IP address. Modern DNS supports record types like A (IPv4), AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), TXT (verification), and SRV (service discovery).

Underneath DNS lies the TCP/IP stack. Data travels through layers: application (HTTP/HTTPS), transport (TCP/UDP), network (IP routing), and link (Ethernet/Wi-Fi frames). TLS handshakes establish encrypted channels using certificate chains rooted in Certificate Authorities. Understanding how packets traverse autonomous systems via BGP, pass through NAT gateways, and arrive at destination servers is fundamental to debugging production issues.

Key concepts include TTL-based caching at every level (browser, OS, ISP resolver), anycast routing used by CDNs and public DNS providers (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1), and DNS propagation delays when records change.`,keyCommands:[{command:"dig example.com +trace",description:"Trace the full DNS resolution path from root to authoritative server"},{command:"nslookup -type=MX example.com",description:"Query specific DNS record types for a domain"},{command:"curl -v https://example.com",description:"Verbose HTTP request showing DNS, TLS handshake, headers, and response"},{command:"traceroute example.com",description:"Show the network hops between your machine and the destination"},{command:"openssl s_client -connect example.com:443",description:"Inspect the TLS certificate chain of a remote server"}],bestPractices:["Set appropriate TTLs: low (60s) before migrations, higher (3600s) for stable records to reduce resolver load","Use CNAME records for subdomains pointing to cloud services; A records for apex domains","Enable DNSSEC to prevent DNS spoofing and cache poisoning attacks","Monitor DNS resolution time as part of your observability stack","Use multiple DNS providers for redundancy (Route53 + Cloudflare)","Prefer HTTPS everywhere and enable HSTS to prevent downgrade attacks"],interviewQuestions:["Walk through every step that happens when you type a URL into a browser and press Enter.","What is the difference between authoritative and recursive DNS servers?","How does DNS caching work and what problems can stale caches cause?","Explain the TLS handshake process. What is the role of Certificate Authorities?","What is anycast routing and why do CDNs use it?"],resources:[{label:"How DNS Works (comic)",url:"https://howdns.works/"},{label:"High Performance Browser Networking",url:"https://hpbn.co/"}]},{id:"request-lifecycle",title:"Request Lifecycle (Browser to DB)",category:"full-stack-lifecycle",order:2,explanation:`A full-stack request lifecycle begins at the browser and ends at the database, passing through multiple layers. The browser resolves DNS, establishes a TCP connection (with TLS for HTTPS), and sends an HTTP request. This request hits a load balancer (ALB/NLB), which routes it to an application server. The app server processes the request through middleware (auth, rate limiting, logging), executes business logic, queries the database, and returns a response.

On the frontend side, the browser parses HTML, builds the DOM, fetches CSS/JS assets (often from a CDN), and renders the page. Single-page applications (SPAs) make subsequent API calls via fetch/XHR, often with JWT tokens in headers or cookies. Understanding CORS, preflight OPTIONS requests, and cookie security attributes (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite) is critical.

The return path is equally important: the response passes back through the load balancer, may be cached at various levels (CDN edge, reverse proxy, application cache, browser cache), and arrives at the client. HTTP status codes, caching headers (Cache-Control, ETag, Last-Modified), and compression (gzip/brotli) all affect performance and correctness.`,keyCommands:[{command:'curl -w "@curl-format.txt" -o /dev/null -s https://api.example.com/health',description:"Measure detailed timing breakdown of an HTTP request"},{command:"httpstat https://api.example.com/health",description:"Visualize HTTP request timing (DNS, TCP, TLS, server, transfer)"},{command:"tcpdump -i eth0 port 443 -w capture.pcap",description:"Capture network packets for deep protocol analysis"},{command:"ab -n 1000 -c 50 https://api.example.com/endpoint",description:"Apache Bench: quick load test with 1000 requests, 50 concurrent"}],bestPractices:["Minimize the number of round trips: use HTTP/2 multiplexing, bundle assets, inline critical CSS","Implement connection pooling for database connections to avoid per-request TCP overhead","Use read replicas and caching layers (Redis/Memcached) to offload the primary database","Add request IDs at the edge and propagate them through every layer for end-to-end tracing","Set proper CORS policies: restrict origins, methods, and headers to what is actually needed"],interviewQuestions:["Describe the full lifecycle of an HTTPS request from the browser to the database and back.","How does a load balancer decide which backend server to route a request to?","What are the differences between connection pooling, keep-alive connections, and multiplexing?","Explain how CORS works and why preflight requests exist.","How would you debug a request that is slow but the server logs show fast response times?"],resources:[{label:"What happens when... (GitHub)",url:"https://github.com/alex/what-happens-when"},{label:"MDN: HTTP Overview",url:"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Overview"}]},{id:"cdn-caching",title:"CDN & Caching",category:"full-stack-lifecycle",order:3,explanation:`A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a globally distributed network of edge servers that cache and serve content close to end users. CDNs reduce latency by eliminating long-haul network trips, offload origin servers by absorbing traffic spikes, and improve availability through redundancy. Major CDN providers include CloudFront (AWS), Cloudflare, Akamai, and Fastly.

Caching operates at multiple layers: browser cache (controlled by Cache-Control and Expires headers), CDN edge cache, reverse proxy cache (Nginx/Varnish), application-level cache (Redis/Memcached), and database query cache. Each layer has different invalidation strategies: TTL-based expiry, manual purge, versioned URLs (cache busting with content hashes), and event-driven invalidation.

The key challenge in caching is cache invalidation. Stale data can cause subtle bugs, while overly aggressive invalidation negates caching benefits. Strategies include write-through (update cache on write), write-behind (async cache update), and cache-aside (application manages cache explicitly). Understanding cache stampede (thundering herd), cache penetration (missing keys hitting DB), and cache avalanche (mass expiry) is crucial for system design interviews.`,keyCommands:[{command:"curl -I https://example.com/asset.js",description:"Inspect HTTP response headers including cache-control directives"},{command:'aws cloudfront create-invalidation --distribution-id EDIST1 --paths "/*"',description:"Invalidate all cached objects in a CloudFront distribution"},{command:'redis-cli SET mykey "value" EX 3600',description:"Set a Redis cache key with a 1-hour TTL"},{command:"redis-cli TTL mykey",description:"Check remaining TTL on a Redis key"},{command:"varnishstat",description:"View Varnish cache hit/miss statistics in real time"}],bestPractices:["Use content-hashed filenames (app.a1b2c3.js) for static assets to enable infinite cache TTLs","Set Cache-Control: no-store for authenticated/personalized responses","Implement cache warming for critical paths to avoid cold-start latency after deployments","Use stale-while-revalidate to serve slightly stale content while fetching fresh data in the background","Monitor cache hit ratios: aim for > 90% for static assets, > 70% for dynamic content"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, and must-revalidate?","How would you design a caching strategy for a social media news feed?","Explain cache stampede (thundering herd) and how to prevent it.","When would you choose Redis over Memcached for caching?","How do CDNs handle cache invalidation across hundreds of edge locations?"],resources:[{label:"MDN: HTTP Caching",url:"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching"},{label:"AWS CloudFront Developer Guide",url:"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/"}]},{id:"load-balancing",title:"Load Balancing",category:"full-stack-lifecycle",order:4,explanation:`Load balancers distribute incoming traffic across multiple backend servers to ensure no single server becomes a bottleneck. They operate at different OSI layers: Layer 4 (transport) balancers route based on IP and port using TCP/UDP, while Layer 7 (application) balancers inspect HTTP headers, URLs, and cookies to make routing decisions. AWS offers ALB (Layer 7, HTTP-aware), NLB (Layer 4, ultra-low latency), and CLB (legacy).

Common load balancing algorithms include round-robin (equal distribution), weighted round-robin (capacity-aware), least connections (route to least busy), IP hash (session affinity), and random with two choices (power of two random choices). Session persistence (sticky sessions) pins a user to a specific backend using cookies, which simplifies stateful applications but can cause uneven distribution.

Health checks are fundamental: the load balancer periodically probes backend servers and removes unhealthy instances from the pool. Configuring appropriate health check paths, intervals, thresholds, and timeouts prevents routing traffic to failed servers while avoiding false positives from slow but healthy servers.`,keyCommands:[{command:"aws elbv2 describe-load-balancers",description:"List all ALBs and NLBs in the current AWS account"},{command:"aws elbv2 describe-target-health --target-group-arn <arn>",description:"Check health status of all targets in a target group"},{command:"nginx -t && nginx -s reload",description:"Test Nginx config syntax and reload without downtime"},{command:"haproxy -c -f /etc/haproxy/haproxy.cfg",description:"Validate HAProxy configuration file"}],bestPractices:["Use Layer 7 (ALB) for HTTP workloads needing path-based routing, host-based routing, or WebSocket support","Use Layer 4 (NLB) for non-HTTP protocols, extreme performance needs, or static IP requirements","Configure proper health check endpoints that verify actual application readiness, not just TCP connectivity","Avoid sticky sessions when possible; store session state in Redis or a database instead","Enable cross-zone load balancing to distribute traffic evenly across availability zones","Set connection draining (deregistration delay) to allow in-flight requests to complete during deployments"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing? When would you choose each?","How do you handle session persistence without sticky sessions?","What happens when a backend server fails? Describe the health check and failover process.","How would you load balance WebSocket connections differently from HTTP requests?","Explain the trade-offs between round-robin, least connections, and consistent hashing algorithms."],resources:[{label:"AWS ELB Documentation",url:"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/"},{label:"Nginx Load Balancing Guide",url:"https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/load-balancer/http-load-balancer/"}]},{id:"api-gateway-reverse-proxy",title:"API Gateway & Reverse Proxy",category:"full-stack-lifecycle",order:5,explanation:`A reverse proxy sits in front of backend servers and forwards client requests to them, providing a single entry point. It handles TLS termination, compression, static file serving, and request buffering. Nginx and HAProxy are the most widely used reverse proxies. An API gateway extends reverse proxy functionality with API-specific features: authentication, rate limiting, request transformation, response aggregation, and API versioning.

AWS API Gateway is a managed service that handles REST and WebSocket APIs at scale. It integrates natively with Lambda for serverless architectures, Cognito for auth, and WAF for security. For microservices, API gateways solve the cross-cutting concerns problem: instead of each service implementing auth, rate limiting, and logging, the gateway handles these centrally.

The Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern uses separate API gateways for different clients (web, mobile, IoT), each tailored to the client's needs. Service mesh solutions like Istio and Linkerd provide similar gateway functionality but at the service-to-service level, handling mutual TLS, retries, circuit breaking, and observability without application code changes.`,keyCommands:[{command:"nginx -T",description:"Dump the full resolved Nginx configuration (all includes merged)"},{command:"aws apigateway get-rest-apis",description:"List all API Gateway REST APIs in the account"},{command:"aws apigatewayv2 get-apis",description:"List all HTTP/WebSocket API Gateway v2 APIs"},{command:'curl -H "x-api-key: KEY" https://api.example.com/v1/resource',description:"Call an API Gateway endpoint with an API key header"},{command:"envsubst < nginx.conf.template > /etc/nginx/nginx.conf",description:"Template Nginx config with environment variables for container deployments"}],bestPractices:["Terminate TLS at the reverse proxy/gateway level; use plain HTTP between internal services","Implement rate limiting per client/API key to protect backends from abuse","Use request/response transformation to decouple frontend contracts from backend schemas","Version APIs via URL path (/v1/, /v2/) or headers, never via query parameters","Configure timeouts at the gateway shorter than backend timeouts to fail fast"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between a reverse proxy, a load balancer, and an API gateway?","How would you implement rate limiting at the API gateway level?","Explain the Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern. When is it useful?","How does an API gateway help with microservices authentication?","What is a service mesh and how does it differ from an API gateway?"],resources:[{label:"AWS API Gateway Developer Guide",url:"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/"},{label:"Nginx Reverse Proxy Guide",url:"https://docs.nginx.com/nginx/admin-guide/web-server/reverse-proxy/"}]},{id:"docker-fundamentals",title:"Docker Fundamentals",category:"docker",order:6,explanation:`Docker uses OS-level virtualization to package applications and their dependencies into lightweight, portable containers. Unlike virtual machines that include a full guest OS, containers share the host kernel and use Linux namespaces (pid, net, mnt, uts, ipc, user) for isolation and cgroups for resource limits. This makes containers start in milliseconds, use minimal memory overhead, and run identically across environments.

A Docker image is a read-only template built from layers. Each instruction in a Dockerfile creates a layer, and layers are cached and shared across images. When a container runs, Docker adds a thin writable layer on top (copy-on-write). Images are stored in registries (Docker Hub, ECR, GCR) and pulled on demand. The union filesystem (OverlayFS) efficiently stacks these layers.

The Docker daemon manages the container lifecycle: build, run, stop, remove. The Docker CLI communicates with the daemon via a REST API over a Unix socket. Containers can expose ports, mount volumes for persistent data, connect to networks, and set environment variables. Understanding the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT, bind mounts vs named volumes, and bridge vs host networking is essential.`,keyCommands:[{command:"docker build -t myapp:1.0 .",description:"Build an image from the Dockerfile in the current directory and tag it"},{command:"docker run -d -p 8080:3000 --name web myapp:1.0",description:"Run a container in detached mode with port mapping"},{command:"docker exec -it web /bin/sh",description:"Open an interactive shell inside a running container"},{command:"docker logs -f --tail 100 web",description:"Follow the last 100 lines of container logs"},{command:"docker system prune -a --volumes",description:"Remove all stopped containers, unused images, and volumes"},{command:'docker inspect web --format "{{.State.Status}}"',description:"Query specific container metadata using Go templates"}],bestPractices:["Use specific image tags (node:20-alpine) instead of :latest for reproducible builds","Run containers as non-root users with USER directive for security","Use .dockerignore to exclude node_modules, .git, and build artifacts from the build context","Prefer COPY over ADD unless you need tar auto-extraction or URL fetching","Set resource limits (--memory, --cpus) in production to prevent noisy-neighbor issues","Use HEALTHCHECK instruction to let Docker monitor container health"],interviewQuestions:["How do containers differ from virtual machines? What are namespaces and cgroups?","Explain the Docker image layer system. How does layer caching work?","What is the difference between CMD and ENTRYPOINT? When would you use each?","How does Docker networking work? Explain bridge, host, and overlay networks.","What happens when a container runs out of memory? How do cgroups enforce limits?"],resources:[{label:"Docker Documentation",url:"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/"},{label:"Docker Deep Dive (Nigel Poulton)",url:"https://www.amazon.com/Docker-Deep-Dive-Nigel-Poulton/dp/1916585256"}]},{id:"dockerfile-best-practices",title:"Dockerfile Best Practices",category:"docker",order:7,explanation:`Writing efficient Dockerfiles is critical for build speed, image size, and security. Multi-stage builds are the most important optimization: use a full SDK image to compile/build, then copy only the binary/artifacts into a minimal runtime image (distroless, alpine, or scratch). This can reduce image sizes from 1GB+ to under 50MB and eliminates build tools from the attack surface.

Layer ordering matters for cache efficiency. Place instructions that change rarely (OS packages, dependencies) before instructions that change frequently (application code). Copy dependency manifests (package.json, go.mod) and install dependencies before copying source code, so dependency installation is cached across builds. Each RUN instruction creates a layer, so chain related commands with && and clean up in the same layer.

Security hardening includes: scanning images with tools like Trivy or Snyk, pinning base image digests (not just tags), running as non-root, dropping capabilities, using read-only root filesystems, and avoiding secrets in build args (use BuildKit secrets instead). Linting tools like hadolint catch common Dockerfile antipatterns before they reach production.`,keyCommands:[{command:"docker build --target builder -t myapp:build .",description:"Build only a specific stage from a multi-stage Dockerfile"},{command:"docker history myapp:1.0",description:"Show the layer history and size contribution of each instruction"},{command:'docker image inspect myapp:1.0 --format "{{.Size}}"',description:"Get the total size of an image in bytes"},{command:"hadolint Dockerfile",description:"Lint a Dockerfile for best practice violations"},{command:"trivy image myapp:1.0",description:"Scan an image for known CVEs and vulnerabilities"},{command:"DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build --secret id=npmrc,src=.npmrc .",description:"Use BuildKit secrets to pass sensitive files without baking them into layers"}],bestPractices:["Use multi-stage builds: compile in a full image, copy only the binary into a minimal runtime image","Order layers from least-frequently to most-frequently changing for maximum cache reuse","Copy dependency manifests first, install dependencies, then copy source code","Chain RUN commands with && and clean up (rm -rf /var/cache/apt) in the same layer to reduce image size","Pin base images by SHA digest for fully reproducible builds across CI runs","Scan images for vulnerabilities in CI/CD before pushing to the registry"],interviewQuestions:["How do multi-stage builds work and why are they important?","How would you optimize a Dockerfile that takes 10 minutes to build?","What is the difference between ARG and ENV in a Dockerfile?","How do you handle secrets during the Docker build process without exposing them in layers?","Explain how layer caching interacts with CI/CD: what breaks the cache and how do you preserve it?"],resources:[{label:"Dockerfile Best Practices",url:"https://docs.docker.com/develop/develop-images/dockerfile_best-practices/"},{label:"Hadolint (Dockerfile linter)",url:"https://github.com/hadolint/hadolint"}]},{id:"docker-compose-networking",title:"Docker Compose & Networking",category:"docker",order:8,explanation:"Docker Compose is a tool for defining and running multi-container applications with a single YAML file. It manages the lifecycle of interconnected services: databases, caches, message queues, and application servers. Each service in a compose file can specify its image/build context, ports, volumes, environment variables, dependencies, and health checks. The `docker compose up` command creates networks, volumes, and starts all services in dependency order.\n\nDocker networking enables containers to communicate. The default bridge network provides DNS-based service discovery within a compose project: containers can reach each other by service name. Custom bridge networks allow cross-project communication. Host networking bypasses Docker networking entirely for maximum performance. Overlay networks span multiple Docker hosts for swarm mode. Macvlan assigns MAC addresses for direct LAN integration.\n\nCompose profiles enable selective service startup (e.g., `--profile debug` includes debug tools). Compose watch enables automatic rebuilds during development. Volume mounts come in three types: bind mounts (host path), named volumes (Docker-managed), and tmpfs (in-memory). Understanding the compose dependency model (depends_on with health check conditions) prevents startup race conditions.",keyCommands:[{command:"docker compose up -d --build",description:"Build images and start all services in detached mode"},{command:"docker compose down -v",description:"Stop and remove containers, networks, and named volumes"},{command:"docker compose logs -f api db",description:"Follow logs from specific services"},{command:"docker compose exec api sh",description:"Open a shell in a running compose service"},{command:"docker network ls",description:"List all Docker networks"},{command:"docker compose --profile debug up",description:"Start services including those in the debug profile"}],bestPractices:["Use depends_on with condition: service_healthy to enforce startup ordering based on health checks","Define named volumes for database data to persist across container restarts","Use .env files for environment variables; never hardcode credentials in compose files","Separate override files (docker-compose.override.yml) for development-specific settings","Use profiles to group optional services (monitoring, debugging, testing)","Pin service image versions in compose files for reproducible environments"],interviewQuestions:["How does Docker DNS-based service discovery work within a compose network?","What is the difference between bind mounts and named volumes? When would you use each?","How do you handle database migration ordering when using docker compose?","Explain Docker networking modes: bridge, host, overlay, macvlan.","How would you set up a development environment with hot-reload using docker compose?"],resources:[{label:"Docker Compose Documentation",url:"https://docs.docker.com/compose/"},{label:"Docker Networking Overview",url:"https://docs.docker.com/network/"}]},{id:"k8s-architecture",title:"Kubernetes Architecture",category:"kubernetes",order:9,explanation:`Kubernetes (K8s) is a container orchestration platform that automates deployment, scaling, and management of containerized applications. The architecture consists of a control plane and worker nodes. The control plane includes: the API server (all communication goes through it), etcd (distributed key-value store for cluster state), the scheduler (assigns pods to nodes based on resource requirements and constraints), and controller managers (reconciliation loops that drive actual state toward desired state).

Worker nodes run the kubelet (agent that manages pod lifecycle), kube-proxy (maintains network rules for service routing), and the container runtime (containerd, CRI-O). When you apply a manifest, the API server validates it, stores it in etcd, the scheduler assigns it to a node, the kubelet on that node pulls the image and starts the container. The controller manager continuously watches for drift between desired and actual state.

Kubernetes follows a declarative model: you describe the desired state in YAML manifests, and controllers reconcile reality to match. This self-healing behavior means K8s automatically restarts crashed containers, reschedules pods from failed nodes, and scales replicas based on load. Understanding the reconciliation loop, custom resource definitions (CRDs), and the operator pattern is key to mastering K8s.`,keyCommands:[{command:"kubectl cluster-info",description:"Display the Kubernetes control plane and CoreDNS addresses"},{command:"kubectl get nodes -o wide",description:"List all nodes with IP addresses, OS, and container runtime info"},{command:"kubectl get componentstatuses",description:"Check health of control plane components (scheduler, controller-manager, etcd)"},{command:"kubectl api-resources",description:"List all available resource types in the cluster"},{command:"kubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp",description:"View cluster events sorted by time for debugging"}],bestPractices:["Run at least 3 control plane nodes across availability zones for high availability","Use etcd backups with regular snapshots and test restoration procedures","Enable RBAC and use least-privilege service accounts for each workload","Use namespaces to organize workloads and apply resource quotas per namespace","Keep the API server secured: enable audit logging, restrict anonymous access, use network policies"],interviewQuestions:["Describe the Kubernetes architecture. What role does each control plane component play?","What happens when you run kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml? Walk through the entire flow.","How does etcd ensure consistency? What happens if an etcd node fails?","Explain the Kubernetes reconciliation loop. How does it achieve self-healing?","What are Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs) and the Operator pattern?"],resources:[{label:"Kubernetes Documentation",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/"},{label:"Kubernetes The Hard Way (Kelsey Hightower)",url:"https://github.com/kelseyhightower/kubernetes-the-hard-way"}]},{id:"k8s-core-objects",title:"Core Objects (Pod, Deployment, Service)",category:"kubernetes",order:10,explanation:`A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, containing one or more containers that share network and storage. Multi-container pods use patterns like sidecar (logging/proxy), init container (setup tasks), and ambassador (proxy to external services). Pods are ephemeral; they are not rescheduled when they die. For reliable workloads, you use higher-level controllers.

A Deployment manages a set of identical pods (ReplicaSet) and handles rolling updates, rollbacks, and scaling. It ensures the desired number of pod replicas are running at all times. The Deployment controller creates new ReplicaSets for updates and gradually shifts traffic. Other workload controllers include StatefulSet (for stateful apps needing stable network identity and persistent storage), DaemonSet (one pod per node for agents/loggers), Job (run-to-completion tasks), and CronJob (scheduled tasks).

A Service provides stable networking for pods. ClusterIP (default) exposes internally, NodePort opens a port on every node, LoadBalancer provisions a cloud LB, and ExternalName maps to a DNS name. Services use label selectors to discover pods. ConfigMaps and Secrets provide configuration injection via environment variables or mounted files, keeping application code environment-agnostic.`,keyCommands:[{command:"kubectl get pods -o wide -n myapp",description:"List all pods in a namespace with node assignment and IP"},{command:"kubectl describe deployment myapp",description:"Show detailed info about a deployment including events and conditions"},{command:"kubectl scale deployment myapp --replicas=5",description:"Manually scale a deployment to 5 replicas"},{command:"kubectl rollout status deployment/myapp",description:"Watch the progress of a rolling update"},{command:"kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp",description:"Roll back to the previous deployment revision"},{command:"kubectl port-forward svc/myapp 8080:80",description:"Forward local port to a service for debugging"}],bestPractices:["Always set resource requests and limits (CPU, memory) for every container","Use readiness probes to prevent traffic from reaching pods that are not ready","Use liveness probes to restart containers that are deadlocked or unresponsive","Store configuration in ConfigMaps and sensitive data in Secrets (or external secret managers)","Use PodDisruptionBudgets to ensure minimum availability during node maintenance","Set pod anti-affinity rules to spread replicas across nodes and zones"],interviewQuestions:["What is the difference between a Pod, a ReplicaSet, and a Deployment?","When would you use a StatefulSet instead of a Deployment?","Explain the different Service types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName.","How do rolling updates work? What are maxSurge and maxUnavailable?","What is the difference between a liveness probe and a readiness probe?"],resources:[{label:"K8s Workload Resources",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/"},{label:"K8s Services, Load Balancing, Networking",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/"}]},{id:"k8s-networking-ingress",title:"Kubernetes Networking & Ingress",category:"kubernetes",order:11,explanation:`Kubernetes networking follows a flat network model: every pod gets its own IP address and can communicate with any other pod without NAT. This is implemented by CNI (Container Network Interface) plugins like Calico, Cilium, Flannel, or AWS VPC CNI. Each plugin handles pod-to-pod networking differently: overlay networks (VXLAN tunnels), direct routing, or native cloud networking.

Services provide stable virtual IPs (ClusterIPs) backed by kube-proxy, which programs iptables or IPVS rules on each node to load-balance traffic to healthy pod endpoints. CoreDNS resolves service names: \`my-svc.my-namespace.svc.cluster.local\`. Headless services (clusterIP: None) return pod IPs directly, useful for StatefulSets and service discovery.

Ingress resources define HTTP routing rules (host-based, path-based) that map external traffic to internal services. An Ingress Controller (Nginx, Traefik, AWS ALB Ingress Controller) implements these rules. The newer Gateway API provides more expressive routing with GRPCRoute, TCPRoute, and traffic splitting. Network Policies act as pod-level firewalls: by default all traffic is allowed, but you can restrict ingress/egress per namespace, pod label, or CIDR block.`,keyCommands:[{command:"kubectl get ingress -A",description:"List all ingress resources across all namespaces"},{command:"kubectl get networkpolicy -n myapp",description:"List network policies in a namespace"},{command:"kubectl get endpoints myservice",description:"Show the pod IPs backing a service"},{command:"kubectl run tmp --image=busybox --rm -it -- wget -qO- http://myservice:8080/health",description:"Test service DNS resolution and connectivity from inside the cluster"},{command:"kubectl get svc -o wide",description:"List services with selectors and external IPs"}],bestPractices:["Use Network Policies to implement least-privilege pod-to-pod communication (default deny, explicit allow)","Terminate TLS at the Ingress controller; use cert-manager for automatic Let's Encrypt certificates","Use the AWS ALB Ingress Controller to provision ALBs directly from Ingress manifests","Prefer the Gateway API over Ingress for new clusters: it supports more protocols and traffic splitting","Monitor DNS resolution and kube-proxy performance as sources of intermittent connectivity issues"],interviewQuestions:["Explain the Kubernetes networking model. How does pod-to-pod communication work?","What is the role of kube-proxy? How does it implement service load balancing?","What is the difference between an Ingress resource and an Ingress Controller?","How do Network Policies work? Can you implement a default-deny-all policy?","Compare Ingress with the newer Gateway API. What problems does Gateway API solve?"],resources:[{label:"K8s Networking Concepts",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/"},{label:"Gateway API",url:"https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/"}]},{id:"k8s-scaling-health",title:"Scaling & Health Checks",category:"kubernetes",order:12,explanation:`Kubernetes supports horizontal and vertical scaling. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) adjusts replica count based on CPU, memory, or custom metrics (requests per second, queue depth). It follows a control loop: every 15 seconds (default), the HPA controller computes desired replicas = ceil(currentMetricValue / targetMetricValue * currentReplicas). The Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) adjusts CPU/memory requests and limits, useful for right-sizing workloads. Cluster Autoscaler adds or removes nodes when pods cannot be scheduled due to insufficient resources.

Health checks come in three types: liveness probes (is the container alive? restart if not), readiness probes (is it ready for traffic? remove from service endpoints if not), and startup probes (has the container started? used for slow-starting apps to avoid premature liveness probe failures). Probes can use HTTP GET, TCP socket, gRPC, or exec commands. Misconfigured probes are a top source of production incidents: too aggressive causes unnecessary restarts, too lenient allows serving errors.

Graceful shutdown is equally important. When K8s terminates a pod, it sends SIGTERM and waits for the terminationGracePeriodSeconds (default 30s) before SIGKILL. The application should handle SIGTERM by stopping new request acceptance, draining in-flight requests, closing database connections, and exiting cleanly. Pre-stop hooks can add a small delay to allow kube-proxy to update routing rules before the pod stops serving.`,keyCommands:[{command:"kubectl autoscale deployment myapp --min=2 --max=10 --cpu-percent=70",description:"Create an HPA targeting 70% CPU utilization"},{command:"kubectl get hpa",description:"Show current HPA status including current vs target metrics and replica counts"},{command:"kubectl top pods -n myapp",description:"Show real-time CPU and memory usage per pod (requires metrics-server)"},{command:"kubectl top nodes",description:"Show resource usage per node"},{command:'kubectl describe pod myapp-xyz | grep -A5 "Conditions"',description:"Check pod conditions including readiness and liveness status"}],bestPractices:["Always set resource requests (for scheduling) and limits (for enforcement) based on observed usage","Use HPA with custom metrics (Prometheus adapter) for application-specific scaling signals","Configure startup probes for applications with long initialization times to avoid liveness probe kills","Set readiness probe initial delay to match application startup time; use failureThreshold for tolerance","Implement graceful shutdown: handle SIGTERM, drain connections, then exit. Set terminationGracePeriodSeconds accordingly","Use PodDisruptionBudgets alongside HPA to ensure minimum availability during scaling events"],interviewQuestions:["How does the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler work? What metrics can it use?","What is the difference between liveness, readiness, and startup probes? Give a scenario for each.","How does Kubernetes handle pod termination? What is the SIGTERM grace period?","How would you scale a workload based on a custom metric like queue depth?","What is the difference between HPA and VPA? Can they be used together?"],resources:[{label:"K8s HPA Walkthrough",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/horizontal-pod-autoscale-walkthrough/"},{label:"K8s Probes Documentation",url:"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/"}]},{id:"pipeline-design",title:"CI/CD Pipeline Design",category:"ci-cd",order:13,explanation:`Continuous Integration (CI) automates building, testing, and validating code on every commit. A well-designed CI pipeline includes stages: lint/format check, compile/build, unit tests, integration tests, security scanning (SAST, dependency audit), and artifact publishing. Each stage provides fast feedback; if any stage fails, the pipeline stops and notifies the developer. CI catches issues early when they are cheap to fix.

Continuous Delivery (CD) extends CI by automating deployment to staging and production environments. The pipeline progresses through environments (dev, staging, production) with gates: automated tests, manual approval, smoke tests, and canary analysis. GitOps takes this further by using Git as the single source of truth for infrastructure and application state; tools like ArgoCD and Flux watch a Git repository and reconcile the cluster state to match.

Pipeline optimization matters at scale. Key techniques include: parallelizing test suites, caching dependencies and Docker layers, using incremental builds (only rebuild what changed), running tests in containers for environment consistency, and implementing test impact analysis to only run tests affected by the change. Popular CI/CD platforms include GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, CircleCI, and Bitbucket Pipelines.`,keyCommands:[{command:"git log --oneline --graph --all",description:"Visualize the branch and merge history"},{command:"docker build --cache-from myapp:latest -t myapp:$SHA .",description:"Build with remote cache for faster CI builds"},{command:"act -j build",description:"Run GitHub Actions workflows locally using act"},{command:"argocd app sync myapp",description:"Trigger ArgoCD to sync application state from Git"},{command:"argocd app diff myapp",description:"Preview changes ArgoCD would apply without syncing"}],bestPractices:["Keep the CI feedback loop under 10 minutes; parallelize tests and cache aggressively","Run linting and fast unit tests first to fail fast; save slow integration tests for later stages","Use ephemeral environments (preview/PR environments) for integration testing and manual review","Tag artifacts with the Git SHA for traceability from deployment back to source code","Implement pipeline-as-code: store CI/CD configuration in the same repository as application code","Use branch protection rules: require CI pass and code review before merge"],interviewQuestions:["Design a CI/CD pipeline for a microservices application. What stages would you include?","What is the difference between continuous delivery and continuous deployment?","How would you handle database migrations in a CI/CD pipeline?","What is GitOps? How does ArgoCD implement the GitOps pattern?","How would you optimize a CI pipeline that takes 30 minutes to run?"],resources:[{label:"GitHub Actions Documentation",url:"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{label:"ArgoCD Getting Started",url:"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/getting_started/"}]},{id:"blue-green-canary",title:"Blue-Green & Canary Deployments",category:"ci-cd",order:14,explanation:`Blue-green deployment maintains two identical production environments: blue (current) and green (new). Traffic is routed entirely to blue while green is deployed and tested. Once verified, traffic switches to green (via DNS, load balancer, or Kubernetes service update). If something goes wrong, instant rollback switches back to blue. The downside is that it requires double the infrastructure during the deployment window.

Canary deployment gradually shifts a small percentage of traffic (1%, 5%, 10%) to the new version while monitoring key metrics (error rate, latency, saturation). If metrics remain healthy, traffic is progressively increased until 100% reaches the new version. If anomalies are detected, traffic shifts back to the old version. This limits the blast radius of bad deployments. Tools like Flagger, Argo Rollouts, and AWS CodeDeploy automate canary analysis.

Other strategies include rolling updates (K8s default: gradually replace old pods with new), A/B testing (route based on user attributes for feature experiments), and shadow/dark deployments (mirror production traffic to the new version without serving responses). Choosing the right strategy depends on risk tolerance, infrastructure cost, and rollback speed requirements.`,keyCommands:[{command:"kubectl set image deployment/myapp web=myapp:v2",description:"Trigger a rolling update by changing the container image"},{command:"kubectl rollout status deployment/myapp",description:"Monitor the progress of a rollout"},{command:"kubectl rollout undo deployment/myapp --to-revision=3",description:"Roll back to a specific deployment revision"},{command:"kubectl argo rollouts set weight myapp 20",description:"Manually set canary traffic weight to 20% using Argo Rollouts"},{command:"kubectl argo rollouts promote myapp",description:"Promote a canary to receive 100% traffic"}],bestPractices:["Define clear rollback criteria: error rate thresholds, latency percentile breaches, or health check failures","Use automated canary analysis (Flagger, Argo Rollouts) to remove human bottlenecks from deployments","Ensure database schema changes are backward-compatible (expand-and-contract migration pattern)","Run smoke tests against the canary before increasing traffic percentage","Monitor both infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory) and business metrics (conversion rate, revenue) during rollouts"],interviewQuestions:["Compare blue-green, canary, and rolling deployments. When would you choose each?","How do you handle database schema changes in a blue-green deployment?","What metrics would you monitor during a canary deployment? How do you automate the decision to promote or rollback?","How does Argo Rollouts implement progressive delivery in Kubernetes?","What is a dark/shadow deployment and when is it useful?"],resources:[{label:"Argo Rollouts Documentation",url:"https://argoproj.github.io/argo-rollouts/"},{label:"AWS Blue/Green Deployments",url:"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/blue-green-deployments/welcome.html"}]},{id:"terraform-basics",title:"Terraform Basics",category:"iac",order:15,explanation:"Terraform by HashiCorp is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool that lets you define cloud resources in declarative HCL (HashiCorp Configuration Language) files. Instead of clicking through the AWS console, you write code that describes your desired infrastructure, and Terraform creates, updates, and destroys resources to match. This enables version control, code review, automated testing, and repeatable environments.\n\nThe Terraform workflow has three phases: `terraform init` (download providers and initialize backend), `terraform plan` (compute the diff between desired and actual state), and `terraform apply` (execute the changes). State is stored in a state file (local or remote backend like S3 + DynamoDB for locking) that maps HCL resources to real-world cloud resources. State management is critical: state drift, state corruption, and concurrent modifications are common pain points.\n\nTerraform modules provide reusable, composable infrastructure components. A module encapsulates a set of resources with input variables and output values. The Terraform Registry hosts community modules for common patterns (VPC, EKS cluster, RDS). Workspaces allow managing multiple environments (dev, staging, prod) from the same configuration. Terraform Cloud and Terragrunt add enterprise features: remote execution, policy enforcement (Sentinel/OPA), cost estimation, and DRY configuration management.",keyCommands:[{command:"terraform init",description:"Initialize the working directory, download providers and modules"},{command:"terraform plan -out=tfplan",description:"Generate and save an execution plan for review"},{command:"terraform apply tfplan",description:"Apply the saved plan to create/update/delete resources"},{command:"terraform state list",description:"List all resources tracked in the state file"},{command:"terraform import aws_instance.web i-1234567890abcdef0",description:"Import an existing resource into Terraform state"},{command:"terraform destroy -target=aws_instance.web",description:"Destroy a specific resource without affecting others"}],bestPractices:["Store state in a remote backend (S3 + DynamoDB) with encryption and locking to prevent concurrent modifications","Use modules for reusable infrastructure patterns; reference versioned modules from a registry or Git","Never store secrets in .tf files or state; use AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, or environment variables","Run terraform plan in CI/CD and require human approval before terraform apply in production","Use workspaces or directory-based separation (envs/dev/, envs/prod/) to manage multiple environments","Pin provider versions to avoid breaking changes from automatic upgrades"],interviewQuestions:["What is Terraform state and why is it important? What problems arise from state management?","How does Terraform differ from CloudFormation? When would you choose one over the other?","Explain the Terraform plan/apply workflow. What is a state lock and why is it needed?","How do you handle secrets in Terraform? What should never be stored in .tf files?","What are Terraform modules? How do you design a module for reusability?"],resources:[{label:"Terraform Documentation",url:"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs"},{label:"Terraform Best Practices",url:"https://www.terraform-best-practices.com/"}]}];export{e as a};
